T3.1 denoise -- three-pixel median vs the reference implementation (x86_64)
================================================================================

PLATFORM: x86_64, native, taskset -c 2, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG), OpenCV 4.5.4.

  INDICATIVE ONLY (EXPERIMENTS.md "Measurement platforms"). NOTHING HERE CLOSES
  AN EXPERIMENT and nothing here is quotable. X-12 closes on the reference
  device; the device log is results/denoise_benchmark_pi4.log.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. EXPERIMENTS.md X-12 used to carry a one-line x86
"for contrast" row with no committed log behind it, unlike every other benchmark
in this directory. That row could not be reproduced -- see the spread below --
so the log is committed and the numbers are quoted from it or not at all.

  sha256[0:16]  79cab91615d5faa5  bincv-cpp/include/bincv-cpp/ops/denoise.hpp
  sha256[0:16]  50fdecde0500c9f4  bincv-cpp/benchmark/denoise_benchmark.cpp

THE SPREAD IS THE POINT. Three consecutive runs of this binary on an idle
machine, 640x480, binCV fused uint32 against the OpenCV denominator:

  run 1   19.25x        (the run printed in full below)
  run 2   16.05x
  run 3   19.34x

20% between the extremes, on the same binary and the same input, because this is
a shared desktop with frequency scaling and no governor control -- exactly what
scripts/run_on_pi.sh exists to eliminate. Read the device log instead. The one
thing this file is good for is the SHAPE that survives the noise: binCV's fused
kernel is more than an order of magnitude faster than the reference at every size
here too, and the ordering of the five rows never changes.

COMMAND

  cmake -S bincv-cpp -B <build> -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build <build> --target denoise_benchmark
  taskset -c 2 <build>/benchmark/denoise_benchmark

================================================================================
T3.1 denoise -- three-pixel median vs the reference implementation
================================================================================

DENOMINATOR (ARCHITECTURE 10.3): SEAL's three_pix_median_filter on the SAME
binary content stored as CV_8U -- cv::min/cv::max over two zero-filled
neighbour matrices, ported call for call. That is what the pipeline runs
today without binCV.

binCV rows: the fused one-pass kernel (ops/denoise.hpp) at uint32 and uint64,
and the composed shiftDown + shiftLeft + majority3 spelling it replaced --
same image, three passes, two frame-sized scratch buffers.

TWO OpenCV ROWS. 'OpenCV CV_8U' re-zeroes only the two neighbour matrices,
whose zeros ARE the border; 'OpenCV as-written' also re-zeroes the four
temporaries the reference builds with cv::Mat::zeros and then completely
overwrites. The DENOMINATOR is the first, so every ratio is conservative by
the gap between them.

The checksum column folds EVERY destination pixel and is representation-
independent, so all rows of a size must print the same value.

Working set is one call's live buffers, not a per-buffer ratio (CLAUDE.md,
ARCHITECTURE 10.4).

FIXED PER-CALL COST (hazard 5), measured on a 2x2 frame -- the part of every
row below that is not the operation:
  OpenCV reference (8 cv:: calls):     0.937 us per call
  binCV denoiseMedian3:                0.003 us per call
  Divided by a frame's pixel count this is what a small frame's ns/pixel is
  mostly made of. It is NOT subtracted from the tables; it is printed beside
  them so the ladder is read with it.

